<% content_for :title, t(".title") %>
<%# See the layout: this is the chat and only the chat. The shell below is a
    fixed height, but a fixed-height shell inside a body that can still grow
    leaves the document draggable behind it.

    `relative` is not decoration and it is not here for a child to position
    against — it is what finishes the job `overflow-hidden` starts. An
    absolutely positioned element is only clipped by an ancestor's overflow
    if that ancestor is in its *containing block* chain, and a containing
    block is the nearest positioned ancestor. Every message bubble carries a
    `.sr-only` sender label, which Tailwind implements as `position:absolute`
    — with nothing on this page positioned, their containing block was the
    initial containing block, so `overflow-hidden` on the body never touched
    them and the label of a message scrolled below the fold sat outside the
    root's client box. Measured: the root reported scrollHeight 721 against
    clientHeight 701 with body itself correctly at 701, and the document
    still scrolled 20px. Making the body the containing block clips them and
    takes it to 0 — proven by toggling this one property in the live page,
    both directions, twice. It is stated on the body rather than on
    #chat-messages so the guarantee covers anything absolutely positioned
    anywhere in the chat, not only today's one label. %>
<% content_for :body_class, "app-shell relative h-dvh overflow-hidden" %>

<%# Current.hotel, not @guest_session.hotel: this line exists specifically
    to make Guest::BaseController#scope_to_guest_hotel's own work visible
    in rendered output, not just GuestSession resolution — see
    test/tenancy/cross_tenant_access_test.rb, which would otherwise only
    ever exercise which session a cookie resolves to, never which tenant
    that session's hotel gets set to.

    chat-scroll/chat-resilience/chat-composer are declared together on one
    element: chat-resilience needs the messages list AND the cable stream
    source in its own scope; chat-composer needs both the quick-action
    chips and the composer form (siblings) in its own scope so a chip tap
    can reach the composer's textarea target. One shared ancestor keeps
    all three Stimulus controllers' target lookups working without any
    inline script. %>
<%# h-dvh, not min-h-dvh. This is the whole reason the composer used to end up
    below the fold: with a minimum height the shell grows to fit the
    transcript, so the *page* scrolls and the input slides off the bottom of
    the screen. Measured on an emulated iPhone with seven messages — the
    composer sat 412px past the fold, which is exactly the "I have to scroll
    back" a guest reports. A fixed height makes the transcript the only thing
    that scrolls and pins the composer where a thumb expects it. %>
<div class="mx-auto flex h-dvh max-w-md flex-col overflow-hidden bg-white"
     data-controller="chat-scroll chat-resilience chat-composer"
     data-chat-resilience-after-url-value="<%= guest_messages_path %>"
     data-chat-resilience-last-id-value="<%= @messages.last&.id.to_i || 0 %>">

  <%# Two lines instead of three. Every pixel here is one the transcript does
      not get, and on a 390px-wide phone the header was taking a fifth of the
      screen to say things the guest already knows — they are standing in the
      hotel. The name stays because a QR code can be scanned in the wrong
      lobby; the room stays because it is what the request will be delivered
      to and getting it wrong is expensive. %>
  <header class="flex shrink-0 items-baseline justify-between gap-3 border-b border-gray-200 px-4 py-2.5">
    <div class="min-w-0">
      <p id="hotel-name" class="truncate text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--brand-primary)"><%= Current.hotel.name %></p>
      <h1 id="chat-greeting" class="truncate text-xs text-gray-500" dir="auto">
        <%= t(".greeting", name: @guest_session.guest_name) %>
      </h1>
    </div>
    <% if @guest_session.room %>
      <p id="chat-room" class="shrink-0 text-xs text-gray-500" dir="auto"><%= t(".room_label", room: @guest_session.room.number) %></p>
    <% end %>
  </header>

  <%# The chat is not an emergency channel — the same notice the landing
      page shows (Guest::EntriesController), reused verbatim rather than a
      second copy of the same guarantee, kept reachable here too. %>
  <p id="not-emergency-notice" class="shrink-0 border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-1.5 text-start text-xs leading-snug text-gray-500" dir="auto">
    <%= t("guest.entries.show.not_emergency_notice") %>
    <% if Current.hotel.contact_phone.present? %>
      <%= link_to Current.hotel.contact_phone, "tel:#{dialable_phone(Current.hotel.contact_phone)}",
            class: "font-medium underline" %>
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <p id="offline-banner" class="hidden bg-amber-50 px-4 py-2 text-center text-xs text-amber-800" role="status"
     data-chat-resilience-target="offlineBanner">
    <%= t("guest.chats.composer.offline_banner") %>
  </p>

  <%# min-h-0 is load-bearing next to flex-1. A flex item defaults to
      min-height:auto, which refuses to shrink below its content — so without
      it this element never becomes a scroller no matter what overflow-y says,
      and the shell grows instead. overscroll-contain stops a flick at the top
      of the transcript from dragging the whole page behind it, which on iOS
      reads as the screen coming loose. %>
  <div id="chat-messages" class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-3 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain px-4 py-4"
       data-chat-scroll-target="messages" data-chat-resilience-target="messages">
    <% if @messages.empty? %>
      <%# A warm greeting from the hotel — never "the AI" or "the
          assistant" (there is no AI in this slice, and won't be a lie
          about one when there is). %>
      <p id="empty-state-greeting" class="rounded-lg bg-gray-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-700" dir="auto">
        <%= t(".empty_state_greeting", hotel_name: Current.hotel.name) %>
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <%= render partial: "guest/messages/message", collection: @messages, as: :message %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <% if @messages.empty? %>
    <%= render "guest/chats/quick_actions", quick_actions: guest_quick_actions(Current.hotel) %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Always rendered, empty when nothing is pending — it is the target of a
      live Turbo replace, and a target that does not exist yet is a broadcast
      that lands nowhere. %>
  <%= render "guest/chats/draft_card", draft: @pending_draft %>

  <%= render "guest/chats/composer" %>

  <%# Signed by Turbo (Turbo.signed_stream_verifier) — a guest cannot mint
      or tamper with this value client-side, and ConversationChannel
      additionally verifies server-side that Current.guest_session actually
      owns the conversation this decodes to before ever confirming the
      subscription (see that channel). %>
  <%= turbo_stream_from @conversation, channel: ConversationChannel, data: { chat_resilience_target: "source" } %>
</div>
